      "content": [
        {
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "The dominant phase indexes to a pseudo-orthorhombic cell with approximately\n\n\\[\na=4.901\\ \\text{\u00c5},\\quad b=7.742\\ \\text{\u00c5},\\quad c=5.914\\ \\text{\u00c5},\n\\]\n\nand is consistent with a **Pnma average structure**, \\(Z=4\\). Representative indexing is 020 at 22.96\u00b0, 101 at 23.56\u00b0, 111 near 26.2\u00b0, 102 at 35.43\u00b0, 200 at 36.65\u00b0, 112 at 37.40\u00b0, 022 near 38.2\u00b0, and 210 near 38.6\u00b0.\n\nThe heavy-atom substructure is well constrained: Re occupies a Pnma 4c site near \\((0.079,\\,1/4,\\,0.096)\\). Cu X-ray data cannot reliably distinguish O from F because their scattering factors are nearly identical. The defensible primary model therefore has a statistically mixed O/F anion site. Its coordinates are less certain than the cell and Re position.\n\n```cif\ndata_ReOF_average\n_audit_creation_method\n'Powder indexing and heavy-atom constrained structure solution from Cu Kalpha1 data'\n_chemical_name_common                 'rhenium oxide fluoride'\n_chemical_formula_structural          'ReOF'\n_chemical_formula_sum                 'Re O F'\n_cell_formula_units_Z                  4\n\n_cell_length_a                         4.9012\n_cell_length_b                         7.7422\n_cell_length_c                         5.9138\n_cell_angle_alpha                      90\n_cell_angle_beta                       90\n_cell_angle_gamma                      90\n_cell_volume                           224.35\n\n_space_group_crystal_system            orthorhombic\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt              'P n m a'\n_space_group_IT_number                 62\n\n_diffrn_radiation_type                 'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength           1.5406\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1  'x,y,z'\n2  'x+1/2,-y+1/2,-z+1/2'\n3  '-x,y+1/2,-z'\n4  '-x+1/2,-y,z+1/2'\n5  '-x,-y,-z'\n6  '-x+1/2,y+1/2,z+1/2'\n7  'x,-y+1/2,z'\n8  'x+1/2,y,-z+1/2'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_U_iso_or_equiv\nRe1  Re  4  c  0.0790  0.2500  0.0965  1.000  0.006\nO1   O   8  d  0.2500  0.1050  0.3000  0.500  0.012\nF1   F   8  d  0.2500  0.1050  0.3000  0.500  0.012\n```\n\nThis should be regarded as the **average powder structure**. Several split or additional reflections\u2014most visibly around 26.1\u201326.3\u00b0 and 37.7\u201338.8\u00b0\u2014suggest weak O/F ordering, a small symmetry-lowering distortion, or a closely related minor domain. The present Cu pattern does not determine a unique ordered O/F alternative; neutron diffraction, anomalous diffraction, or chemical constraints would be needed to choose among such subgroups.",
